================================================================================
The OCR runtime is NOT part of this project
================================================================================

Tank-OCR performs no text recognition of its own. It is a local HTTP server
that calls the Chrome Screen AI runtime through FFI.

That runtime — the library (chrome_screen_ai.dll / libchromescreenai.so) and
its model files (*.binarypb, *.tflite) — is produced and distributed by Google
as a Chrome component. It is:

  * NOT included in this repository
  * NOT covered by the MIT license above
  * governed by Google's own terms and by the licenses listed in the
    THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES file that ships inside the component directory

The component carries a mix of licenses (Apache-2.0, BSD variants, MPL, and
GPL-with-exception among others). This project therefore does not redistribute
it. It is read at runtime from wherever Chrome already installed it:

    Windows  %LOCALAPPDATA%\Google\Chrome\User Data\screen_ai\<version>\
    macOS    ~/Library/Application Support/Google/Chrome/screen_ai/<version>/
    Linux    ~/.config/google-chrome/screen_ai/<version>/

If you fork or redistribute Tank-OCR, do not bundle that directory. Users
obtain it through their own Chrome installation. Reference:
https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/services/screen_ai/README.md

================================================================================
Third-party code
================================================================================

clv-locro — https://github.com/sergiocorreia/clv-locro
    MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Sergio Correia

    The FFI call sequence used to drive the Screen AI library (symbol names,
    the file-content callback protocol, and the Skia bitmap layout expected by
    PerformOCR) follows this project's Python implementation. No source is
    copied into this repository; the Go code in engine/screenai.go is an
    independent implementation of the same interface.

github.com/ebitengine/purego — Apache-2.0
    Calls the runtime library without cgo.

github.com/ledongthuc/pdf — MIT
    Extracts the native text layer from PDFs, so pages that already contain
    text are not sent through OCR.

golang.org/x/image — BSD-3-Clause
    Image decoding and scaling.

Full dependency terms resolve through the Go module graph; see go.mod.

================================================================================
Optional fallback engine
================================================================================

fallback/ contains a small Python service that exposes PaddleOCR over the same
HTTP shape, for machines with no Chromium-based browser. PaddleOCR and its
models are licensed by their own authors (Apache-2.0) and are likewise not
included here.
